{
"dur": 37.199,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 3.4,
"text": "Then came medieval Europe, and the New Year's party got cancelled.",
"end": 3.675
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 3.675,
"vo_start": 3.95,
"vo_end": 11.39,
"text": "Church leaders saw it as un-Christian, and swapped it for holidays like Christmas. Which, funnily enough, had pagan roots of its own.",
"end": 11.665
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 11.665,
"vo_start": 11.94,
"vo_end": 16.53,
"text": "For a while, January the first was just... an ordinary day.",
"end": 16.805
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 16.805,
"vo_start": 17.08,
"vo_end": 25.431,
"text": "Then, in 1582, Pope Gregory the Thirteenth stepped in. The calendar had drifted away from the sun again, so he created a new one: the Gregorian calendar.",
"end": 25.706
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 25.706,
"vo_start": 25.981,
"vo_end": 30.859,
"text": "To catch up, it jumped forward ten days. And New Year's Day was back on January the first.",
"end": 31.134
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 31.134,
"vo_start": 31.409,
"vo_end": 36.249,
"text": "Many Catholic countries in Europe adopted it quickly. Others took a lot longer.",
"end": 37.199
}
],
